  "theory": {
    "intro": "When the numerator of an algebraic fraction is a binomial such as $x+3$, it behaves as a single quantity. Keep it in brackets while you rewrite the fractions over a common denominator, then expand and collect like terms.",
    "facts": [
      "To make an equivalent fraction, multiply **every term** of the numerator by the same factor as the denominator: $\\frac{4x+7}{3} = \\frac{5(4x+7)}{15} = \\frac{20x+35}{15}$.",
      "Scaling down works the same way: $\\frac{3x+9}{18} = \\frac{x+3}{6}$, dividing every term by $3$.",
      "Once the denominators match, combine the numerators over the one denominator: $\\frac{a}{n} \\pm \\frac{b}{n} = \\frac{a \\pm b}{n}$.",
      "**Bracket a binomial numerator before subtracting.** $\\frac{x-1}{3} - \\frac{x+4}{5} = \\frac{5(x-1) - 3(x+4)}{15}$ — the minus sign multiplies **every** term of the second numerator.",
      "So $-3(x+4) = -3x-12$ and $-6(3-x) = -18+6x$: expanding a negative across a binomial changes the sign of each term.",
      "Finish by collecting like terms in the numerator, and check whether the whole fraction has a common factor: $\\frac{4x+8}{6} = \\frac{2x+4}{3}$.",
      "A negative sign can sit with the fraction or with the numerator: $-\\frac{a+b}{c} = \\frac{-a-b}{c}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Rewrite the fractions with the lowest common denominator.",
      "Add or subtract the numerators.",
      "Expand and simplify."
    ]
  },
